"executive_summary": "This paper investigates the Cauchy problem for a class of two-dimensional nonlinear nonlocal wave equations that model anti-plane shear motions in elastic materials. The nonlocal nature is characterized by a convolution integral in the spatial variables, where the Fourier transform of the kernel is nonnegative and satisfies a specific growth condition at infinity. The primary objective is to establish the well-posedness of the initial value problem, as well as conditions for global existence or finite-time blow-up of solutions for a wide class of kernel functions. The study extends previous one-dimensional techniques to the two-dimensional setting. The authors derive the model from the theory of nonlocal elasticity, where stress at a point depends on the strain field throughout the body, addressing limitations of conventional elasticity theories regarding small-scale effects. For initial data in L2 Sobolev spaces, local existence and uniqueness are proven using Banach space-valued ordinary differential equation methods. Global existence is established under a positivity condition on the nonlinear function F and sufficient smoothness of initial data, utilizing the conservation of energy. Conversely, finite-time blow-up is proven when the initial energy is negative and the nonlinearity satisfies a specific growth condition. The results highlight the regularizing effect of the nonlocal kernel, with higher smoothness parameters allowing for weaker regularity requirements on initial data. The paper concludes that blow-up can occur even for small initial data if the potential energy is sufficiently negative, providing a comprehensive analysis of the dynamic behavior of these nonlocal elastic systems.",
